    "md5": "022a4922367e05fbaa738a1c3ae63311",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1499,
    "chunk_text": "He said, \u201cI just want to raise a point of Order. The Determination violates the powers of this House given under Article 4 of the Constitution\" and was referring mainly to the Determination on the Gender Equality Bill and some of the provisions therein. Thereafter, he said, \u201cThe Supreme Court cannot rule on a Bill; they can only advise us\u201d. Then, he also referred to the 1972 Constitution, which really surprised me - referring to history is fine - and said how the 1978 Constitution came up from it. But, if you all are looking at a precedent, then you should go beyond that, not just look at a precedent which is only there.\n\nThen, it goes without saying that when a pre-enactment review of a Bills is needed, it is referred to the Supreme Court and they review it, look at it constitutionally to see whether it is in line with the Constitution or not and gives its Constitutional interpretation. Any perceived, genuine issue in the Determination in terms of accuracy must necessarily be undertaken through an appropriate reference to the court and not through a Select Committee of Parliament. The President wants to refer it to a Select Committee of Parliament. We should go through the legal process, use the system that is there. Taking any other step would be contrary to the due respect of the Judiciary or for judicial independence, and that is what a responsible Legislature should avoid. So, it is very unfortunate that the President came and said such a thing here in this House."
  },
