    "chunk_text": "Then, it goes without saying that when a pre-enactment review of a Bills is needed, it is referred to the Supreme Court and they review it, look at it constitutionally to see whether it is in line with the Constitution or not and gives its Constitutional interpretation. Any perceived, genuine issue in the Determination in terms of accuracy must necessarily be undertaken through an appropriate reference to the court and not through a Select Committee of Parliament. The President wants to refer it to a Select Committee of Parliament. We should go through the legal process, use the system that is there. Taking any other step would be contrary to the due respect of the Judiciary or for judicial independence, and that is what a responsible Legislature should avoid. So, it is very unfortunate that the President came and said such a thing here in this House.\n\n\u201cIn the Republic of Sri Lanka sovereignty is in the People and is inalienable. Sovereignty includes the powers of government, fundamental rights and the franchise.\u201d\n\nThen, the Article 4. (c) states, I quote:\n\n\u201cthe judicial power of the People shall be exercised by Parliament ...\"\n\nBut, that should be,\n\n\"...through courts, tribunals and institutions created and established, or recognized, by the Constitution, \u2026.\u201d\n\nSo, I think this balance needs to be understood; for sure, it needs to be understood and needs to be upheld. We must not go on undermining it in anyway. Therefore, it is pertinent for us to remind ourselves that we have a responsibility. We look to the Supreme Court to get the constitutionality of issues properly considered and determined. That is what it is there for and that role cannot be taken over by the Legislature. There is separation of powers and we must maintain that. So, it is really worrying that the President said that. We know that there are the concepts of Parliamentary supremacy and constitutional supremacy."
  },
