    "chunk_size_bytes": 1502,
    "chunk_text": "of the Supreme Court clearly articulated and told him to read Article 30(2), which had been amended by the Nineteenth Amendment and which very specifically says that the term of the President is five years. Then while that matter was there, the State tended to quote a document stating to be an announcement by the incumbent President saying that he believes that the term is only five years. I also appeared in that matter and it seemed rather curious why the incumbent President should say that at all. Nobody asked for his opinion on the matter. The matter was there for judicial interpretation and this letter was given.\n\nWhen the Nineteenth Amendment to the Constitution was presented to this House, it was the then Prime Minister Ranil Wickremesinghe who moved it. It was announced that only the amendments which do not require a Referendum were to be done at that stage because we were then moving to prepare a new Constitution for the country and because Article 83(a) of the Constitution was listed as one of the Articles which require a Referendum if it was to be amended, Article 83 was not touched. But, that did not mean anything at all, as to what the term of the Presidency or what the term of the Parliament were to be after the Nineteenth Amendment took effect because Articles 30(2) and 62 (2) were both amended by the Nineteenth Amendment to the Constitution, decisively changing the term from six years to five years and that is what is in existence as the law of the country today."
  },
