    "chunk_size_bytes": 1271,
    "chunk_text": "When the Nineteenth Amendment to the Constitution was presented to this House, it was the then Prime Minister Ranil Wickremesinghe who moved it. It was announced that only the amendments which do not require a Referendum were to be done at that stage because we were then moving to prepare a new Constitution for the country and because Article 83(a) of the Constitution was listed as one of the Articles which require a Referendum if it was to be amended, Article 83 was not touched. But, that did not mean anything at all, as to what the term of the Presidency or what the term of the Parliament were to be after the Nineteenth Amendment took effect because Articles 30(2) and 62 (2) were both amended by the Nineteenth Amendment to the Constitution, decisively changing the term from six years to five years and that is what is in existence as the law of the country today.\n\n2049 2050\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca \u0dc3\u0dc1\u0db8\u0dca\u0dc0\u0dd2\u0daf\u0dad\u0dd4\n\nunnecessary, an absolutely unnecessary exercise that the Cabinet is moving to do, unnecessary for purposes of understanding what the term of office of the President is, but necessary for the incumbent, necessary for the incumbent to confuse and say that Article 83(b) needs to be amended before the Presidential Election is to be conducted."
  },
  {
